<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Navarre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of Navarre was a small Christian kingdom in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. Its forces joined the Castilian and Aragonese armies that defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212. It is mentioned in the Almohad profile in connection with this coalition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Navarre" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguillen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_de_Reino_de_Navarra.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The combined armies of Castile, Aragon and <b>Navarre</b>, joined by Portuguese and other crusading contingents, shattered the Almohad force; al-Nasir fled the field and died in Marrakesh the following year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The French rights descended from the counts of Foix to the kings of <b>Navarre</b>; when Henry III of Navarre became Henry IV of France, his edict of 1607 joined them to the French crown, and they later passed to the French heads of state, from emperors to the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The monarchy was a composite state: Castile, Aragon, Valencia, Catalonia, <b>Navarre</b>, Naples, Sicily, Milan, the Netherlands, the Americas and, from 1580, Portugal each kept its own laws, and the king ruled the non-Castilian kingdoms through viceroys and governors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the tenth century it passed by marriage to the kings of Pamplona (<b>Navarre</b>).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Alfonso VI and Alfonso VII used the imperial title, imperator totius Hispaniae, to claim leadership over the other Christian rulers, and in 1135 Alfonso VII was crowned emperor in León, receiving homage from the king of <b>Navarre</b>, the count of Barcelona and Muslim princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings of Castile, Aragon and <b>Navarre</b> routed the Almohad caliph's army in the Sierra Morena.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-16">16 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
